{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790464637,"nonce":"oeNRlGlfHhAt/SRk2YrOWRAg6E4=","message":"2026-09-27, found while linking: caribou 17b3a9e makes a Haxe program's calls into a plugin direct references to the plugin's link symbols. Ash's linker then names exactly those symbols as unresolved, as long as the plugin isn't in the link. A linked plugin cannot be a second Rust staticlib beside the runtime object: two separately built Rust staticlibs duplicate std and the allocator. Nor can it be the plugin crate's object alone, whose references to caribou_abi and other dependencies carry another build's symbol hashes. So linked mode builds one crate graph per program: caribou-runtime plus the program's linked plugins, as one staticlib, then prelinked as today. It needs the Rust toolchain and the plugins' crates at build time; a program with no linked plugin keeps the prebuilt runtime object. Side-module mode (ce0bba4) has no such constraint: each side module has its own std, as ash's Rust HDLLs do.","files":null}]}